                <pb facs="0990" n="876" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg876"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.802 Lloyd Brasileiro Navigation Co./24:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Morgan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rio de Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-04-29T15:00:00-03:00">April 29, 1931—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 29—2:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>24. Department’s telegram No. 20, March 20, 6 p.m. and No. 23, April 14, 3
                        p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d743-f1"/> Foreign Minister states that favors
                    contained decree 19682 are given as a form of subvention to Lloyd Brasileiro,
                    nearly all the shares of which belong to Federal Government; also that the said
                    decree does not establish favors at Brazilian ports to <pb facs="0991" n="877"
                        xml:id="pg877"/> Brazilian vessels nor does it create difference of
                    treatment between ships of this or that flag nor restrict at all the rights of
                    foreign companies. He believes that the reduction of fees collected by Brazilian
                    consuls from shippers who give preference to shipments by the Lloyd Brasileiro
                    boats results from legitimate act of sovereignty and cannot injure the rights of
                    other nationals.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Morgan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
